$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 06/Source population

::EPI-W6-013::A useful control-selection question is: {
=Would this person have had a reasonable chance to be identified as a case if they developed the outcome?#Correct.
~Does this person have exactly the same age as every case?#Review case-control sampling logic.
~Can this person remember every exposure?#Review case-control sampling logic.
~Does this person live furthest from the cases?#Review case-control sampling logic.
}

$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 06/Odds ratio

::EPI-W6-014::In a case-control table, exposed cases\=10, unexposed cases\=8, exposed controls\=20, unexposed controls\=30. What is the odds ratio ad/bc? {
=1.88#Correct. OR equals exposed cases × unexposed controls divided by unexposed cases × exposed controls.
~1.58#This treats sampled case/control totals like risks; standard case-control sampling does not support a directly estimated risk ratio.
~0.13#Use the cross-product formula ad/bc.
~1.39#Use the cross-product formula ad/bc.
}

