$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 06/Bias

::EPI-W6-034::Why can hospital controls create bias? {
=Their reason for hospital attendance may be related to the exposure#Correct.
~Hospital controls always have the outcome#Review case-control sampling logic.
~They prevent calculation of any odds ratio#Review case-control sampling logic.
~They make sample size infinite#Review case-control sampling logic.
}

::EPI-W6-035::Cases remember a past food exposure more carefully than controls. What is the main concern? {
=Recall bias#Correct.
~Random error only#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~Effect modification#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~No epidemiological concern#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
}

::EPI-W6-036::Interviewers probe cases more intensely because they know the hypothesis. What is the main concern? {
=Interviewer information bias#Correct.
~Random error only#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~Effect modification#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~No epidemiological concern#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
}

::EPI-W6-037::Controls are selected from a clinic whose patients commonly have the exposure. What is the main concern? {
=Selection bias#Correct.
~Random error only#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~Effect modification#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~No epidemiological concern#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
}

::EPI-W6-038::Cases are defined partly by having the suspected exposure. What is the main concern? {
=Information/design bias from non-independent case definition#Correct.
~Random error only#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~Effect modification#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~No epidemiological concern#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
}

::EPI-W6-039::Exposure records are missing more often for controls than cases. What is the main concern? {
=Differential information bias#Correct.
~Random error only#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~Effect modification#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~No epidemiological concern#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
}

::EPI-W6-040::In an outbreak investigation, cases remember a past food exposure more carefully than controls. What is the main concern? {
=Recall bias#Correct.
~Random error only#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~Effect modification#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~No epidemiological concern#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
}

::EPI-W6-041::In a rare-cancer study, interviewers probe cases more intensely because they know the hypothesis. What is the main concern? {
=Interviewer information bias#Correct.
~Random error only#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~Effect modification#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~No epidemiological concern#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
}

::EPI-W6-042::In a hospital case-control study, controls are selected from a clinic whose patients commonly have the exposure. What is the main concern? {
=Selection bias#Correct.
~Random error only#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~Effect modification#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~No epidemiological concern#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
}

::EPI-W6-043::In an occupational study, cases are defined partly by having the suspected exposure. What is the main concern? {
=Information/design bias from non-independent case definition#Correct.
~Random error only#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~Effect modification#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~No epidemiological concern#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
}

::EPI-W6-044::In a foodborne-disease study, exposure records are missing more often for controls than cases. What is the main concern? {
=Differential information bias#Correct.
~Random error only#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~Effect modification#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
~No epidemiological concern#Identify whether selection or measurement differs systematically by group.
}

$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 06/Interpretation

::EPI-W6-045::Why can risk generally not be estimated directly from a standard case-control sample? {
=The investigator selects the numbers of cases and controls#Correct.
~Cases never have exposures#Review case-control sampling logic.
~Controls are followed prospectively#Review case-control sampling logic.
~Odds cannot be calculated#Review case-control sampling logic.
}

::EPI-W6-046::An odds ratio is 4.0 in a case-control study of a rare disease. Which wording is safest? {
=Cases had four times the odds of prior exposure compared with controls#Correct.
~Exposed people had exactly four times the risk of disease#Use odds language and avoid causal overstatement.
~Exposure caused four times more disease#Use odds language and avoid causal overstatement.
~Disease prevalence was 400% higher#Use odds language and avoid causal overstatement.
}

